## 3. What Is Advanced Java? (JEE / J2EE / Jakarta EE)

To build a web application, you need to learn:

- **Servlets** – Java classes that handle HTTP requests/responses on the server side.
- **JSP (Java Server Pages)** – Allows embedding Java code inside HTML for dynamic web pages.
- **EJB (Enterprise Java Beans)** – Used for building scalable, transactional, distributed enterprise components.
- **Java Server Faces** and other enterprise-level frameworks.

All of these together form what is called **Advanced Java**, also known as:

> **JEE = Java Enterprise Edition** (older name)
**Jakarta EE** (current name)
> 

### History / Naming Timeline (Common Interview Trivia)

| Year | Event |
| --- | --- |
| ~1995 (1990s) | Java released by **Sun Microsystems** |
| 1997 | Advanced Java released, originally called **J2EE** (Java 2 Enterprise Edition) |
| 2005 | Renamed from **J2EE** to **JEE** (Java Enterprise Edition) |
| ~2009–2010 | Sun Microsystems ran into financial trouble and was **acquired by Oracle** |
| ~2018–2019 | Oracle transferred ownership of the Enterprise Edition specification to the **Eclipse Foundation** |
| Post-2019 | Renamed again to **Jakarta EE** (because Oracle retained rights to the "Java" trademark, so the Eclipse Foundation had to rebrand it) |

**Key point for interviews:**

- **Core Java** is still governed under the "Java" brand (originally Sun → now Oracle).
- **Enterprise/Advanced Java** is now maintained by the **Eclipse Foundation** and officially called **Jakarta EE**.
- You may hear people casually say "J2EE" or "JEE" — all refer to the same enterprise technology stack, just different eras/names. Interviewers rarely quiz you deeply on this trivia; they care more about your understanding of the underlying logic (Servlets, JSP, etc.) than the naming history.

