## 9. Quick Recap Table – Key Terms Glossary (High-Value for Interviews)

| Term | Definition |
| --- | --- |
| **Server** | A computer with server software installed, capable of handling requests and sending responses |
| **Server Software** | Software (e.g., Apache Tomcat) that processes HTTP requests/responses |
| **Data Center** | A physical facility housing many server machines |
| **IP Address** | Unique numeric address identifying a machine on a network |
| **Port Number** | Identifies a specific application running on a server machine (e.g., 8080, 443) |
| **DNS (Domain Name System)** | Translates human-readable domain names (amazon.com) into IP addresses |
| **Standalone Application** | Runs locally, no internet required (e.g., Notepad) |
| **Web Application** | Accessed remotely via browser + internet (e.g., Amazon, Zomato) |
| **Servlet** | Java class that handles requests/responses on the server side (core building block of Java web apps) |
| **JSP (Java Server Pages)** | Allows embedding Java code within HTML for dynamic content |
| **EJB (Enterprise Java Beans)** | Framework for scalable, distributed enterprise-level components |
| **J2EE / JEE / Jakarta EE** | Historical names for the same "Advanced Java" enterprise specification, now maintained by the Eclipse Foundation |
| **Dynamic Web Project** | Eclipse project type used to build Java web applications (vs. a plain Java Project) |
| **web.xml** | Deployment descriptor file that configures welcome files, servlet mappings, etc. |
| **WebContent / webapp folder** | Where all client-facing files (HTML, JSP, CSS) must be placed |
| **WEB-INF** | Contains configuration files (like web.xml) and compiled classes — not directly accessible by clients |
| **localhost** | Refers to your own machine (used when client and server are the same system during development) |
| **HTTP 404** | "Not Found" — indicates the server connection is working, but the specific resource/page doesn't exist |

