## 10. Likely Interview Questions & Model Answers

**Q1: What is the difference between Core Java and Advanced Java?**

> Core Java covers language fundamentals — OOP, exceptions, collections, multithreading, and basic database connectivity (JDBC). Advanced Java (Jakarta EE) builds on top of this to enable building web applications, using technologies like Servlets, JSP, and EJB.
> 

**Q2: What is a server, in your own words?**

> A server is a regular computer that has server software installed on it (like Apache Tomcat), which allows it to receive requests from clients and send back responses. Without server software, even the most powerful hardware is not technically a "server."
> 

**Q3: Why is a port number needed if we already have an IP address?**

> An IP address identifies the machine, but a single machine can run multiple applications simultaneously. The port number tells the server exactly which application on that machine should handle the incoming request.
> 

**Q4: What is the role of DNS?**

> DNS acts as a directory service that converts human-friendly domain names (like google.com) into machine-readable IP addresses, so users don't have to memorize numeric addresses.
> 

**Q5: What is the default welcome file in a Java web project, and can it be changed?**

> By default, it's `index.html` (or `index.jsp`). It can be changed by editing the `<welcome-file-list>` element inside the `web.xml` deployment descriptor.
> 

**Q6: Why is Java EE now called Jakarta EE?**

> After Oracle acquired Sun Microsystems and later transferred governance of the Enterprise Edition specification to the Eclipse Foundation, Oracle retained the "Java" trademark, so the specification was rebranded as "Jakarta EE" to reflect the new governing body.
> 

**Q7: Where should HTML files be placed in a Dynamic Web Project, and why?**

> They must be placed directly inside the `WebContent`/`webapp` folder — not inside `WEB-INF` or `META-INF` — because those subfolders are reserved for configuration and metadata, and files placed there are not served directly to clients.
> 

